  "investigation_notes": "Uploads failing due to filesystem **inode exhaustion** on upload storage \u2014 not app, permission, or DB failure.\n\n* App `incidentlab-app-1` is `running` (E1,E6) but `/health` returns `503 unhealthy` (E2).\n* App logs show upload and health-probe writes failing with `OSError: [Errno 28] No space left on device: '/data/tmp/...part'` and `'/data/uploads/.health-...'` (E3).\n* Storage filesystem `/srv/lab-data` \u2014 bind-mounted to `/data` `rw` (E6) \u2014 has block space free: `37.3%` used, `282.9 MB` available (E4), but `inode_use_percent: 100.0%` (E4). Root `/` is fine `6.8%` used, inodes `1.5%` (E8), so failure is isolated to `/srv/lab-data` inodes, explaining `ENOSPC` despite free MBs.\n* Permissions ruled out: `/srv/lab-data/uploads` (E5) and `/srv/lab-data` (E11) owned `1000:1000` `0755`, app runs as `1000:1000` (E6), `app_user_can_write: true`.\n* DB dependency ruled out: `incidentlab-db-1` `running (healthy)` (E1,E10), logs `database system is ready to accept connections` (E7), `pg_isready` `exit_code 0` repeatedly (E9).\n* Recent `stop/die/start` at `23:48:56` (E9) did not recover; containers `Up 20s/26s` (E1).\n\n**Root cause:** no free inodes on filesystem holding `/srv/lab-data/uploads`.\n**Symptoms:** upload `ENOSPC` errors, `503` health check.\n",
